> From: "Timothy C. May" <tcmay@netcom.com>

>> Let's say the numbers are populations of cities taken at random from a 
>> gazetteer. In this case I believe Tim would lose by having to pay out on
>> numbers starting with 1, 2, 3 or 4.
>>
>> I make the true odds as: 
>>                             1, 2, 3 or 4 ............. 1.512 / 1 on
>>                             5, 6, 7, 8 or 9 .......... 1.512 / 1 against

> I don't follow this. The odds are not even. There are actually _more_
> numbers (from the sources I listed in the original post) beginning with 1,
> 2, 3, or 4 than those beginning with the remaining five digits. This is
> counterintuitive, but is true.

This demonstrates the hazards of not showing the working out and method --
make a minor miscalculation and you get branded just like a miscreant who got
the whole method hopelessly wrong.  :-)

I haven't said that the intuitive view is right. I deliberately omitted my
workings / explanation, so as not to spoil the fun if several differing
interpretations came up. Actually I made an error in taking the log of 4
rather than the log of 5 but I did correctly conclude that there are more
1, 2, 3 or 4 numbers. I made it 60.2% which expressed as odds is 1.512 / 1
on. My latest calculations say the 1/2/3/4 distribution should be 69.9%,
true odds of 2.322 / 1 on. And this is borne out by my observations.

Here's my reasoning, method, and (slightly revised) calculations: 
Populations tend to multiply. Maybe everything has a logarithmic distribution
anyway except random numbers, but if anything does, population size is an 
obvious candidate. So I checked it out by looking up a few population figures
(from 2 pages of a gazetteer, alphabetical order listing). Ignoring the decimal
point, those beginning with 1/2/3/4 came up 55 times, but the 5/6/7/8/9s only
came up 22 times. This didn't fit my earlier calculations, but now looks about
right. 

Since the midway point on a log scale in each decade is root 10 [ * 10 ^ EE ],
I'd expect half the distribution to be between 1 and 3.162, and half from
3.162 to 9.999. Intuition would suggest that 1/2/3/4 come up less than
5/6/7/8/9. I think 1 thru' 4 would come up 69.9% of the time, and 5 thru' 9
30.1% of the time. As the log of 5 is 0.699.

>> By obtaining a price of evens for the 1/2/3/4 hits I reckon the opponent
>> would have secured a return of around 20.4% for himself, on total turnover.
>> And he is laying the 5/6/7/8/9 at only evens when it should be around 6 / 4.

So now I make it that the person taking the 1/2/3/4 side of the bet is on to
an even better (no puns intended) thing than this. Getting 69.9% hits at even 
money yields a profit of 39.8% on stakes invested.

>> However, in the case of number of pages in books, size of postings, etc, I
>> think Tim would have the odds in his favour, an 11.1% return.

> No, actually, I would've lost rather badly.

> Timothy C. May

I thought that certain classes of things might have a linear distribution,
but it seems that truly random decimal numbers are the only class. I've just
been looking at sizes of files on my hard disk (ranked in order of size), and
can confirm that the log distribution certainly applies here too. 

This is how I explain it: Consider what happens as you cross over into the 
next decade. The numbers count up 998, 999, 1000, 1001, 1002... and you've
got ten times as much to go through to get from 1000 to 1010 as from 999
to 1000. But the decimal system of notation is an artificial construct. There
is nothing special about numbers starting 100* compared to 99* that warrants 
an extra significant digit. Every time Nature 'generates' a new size (of 
anything), she does so on a logarithmic scale. When you convert from this
scale to the artificial decimal system of notation, the numbers at the lower
end of a decade are progressively more likely to come up, in exactly the same
proportion as the spacings on a slide rule.

No, I'm not going to support 'Nature'. I'd rather start up an organization
'Support Your Local Logarithms'! 

