Not quite, yes we suffer taxation, and yes the churches are exempted from
that suffering. 

However the part you didn't carry over in your reply was that there are
stipulations in the law that prohibit churches from certain activities
in order to *earn* that exemption. Those churches which enjoy their exemption,
but disregard the prohibitions should be held accountable in my view.
Do you see it otherwise?

 > Murray Rothbard characterized such an attitude this way:
 > Suppose you are part of a group of people held hostage and forced to labor
 > by a criminal gang.  One of your party escapes to freedom.  What sort of
 > person would cheer the criminals' efforts to recapture the escapee?  The
 > appropriate response is to salute the his good fortune and to redouble your
 > efforts to throw off your own shackles.
 
A limited scenario, of the sort I would only expect from the naive!

how about the escapee that does so by climbing over your back, preventing you
from doing so yourself, I suppose you'd salute his superior wit and skill
despite the potential for your own imminent punishment!
 Or how about the added burden placed on the remaining  prisoners due to the
escape.


Simply put Tom, nobodys holding a gun to your head to stay, or to remain in 
the system. You can take your chances with any number of schemes, or
dodges. This is just the sort of anti-social *let the suckers pay* attitude
that we got from Leona Helmsly and Marie Antoinette.
I think it is just the sort of anti-social attitude that keeps
libertarian principles from gaining credence in the general population
as well.

I don't say its not a valid way of thinking, but i am saying that you better
think it through before you take on an adversary with that attitude.
And that thinking better be well tempered by common sense as well as theory.

Last week I had an interesting encounter in the local market.
After waiting too long in the middle of a long line, the aisle next to me
opened up, as I was moving my shopping cart over to take advantage of teh 
opening a young guy on the end of the line was moving in quickly to 
cut others off. I suggested to him that it might be more fair to go
in the order we had already established, and his reply was *life isn't fair*.
I quickly assesed that I was easily twice his size, and probably lots meaner,
adn said *you're right and pushed his cart back into his midsection as I 
noted that I was *lots* bigger than he was. He took his place at the *end*
of the new line without a sound.

In a similar example,
The Davidians may have gotten their rush by winning that first battle, but 
essentially they are now dead meat.

LUX ./. owen

